#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""accept --isabelle-version <v> --isabelle-build <b> --top <package> [--component <package>] [options] --channel <url>...

§6 of the patch tool plan on one platform leg (decision 11's accept job): every scenario starts from
a new environment, and every package that must stay is asked for explicitly.  The channels are the
batch's local channel, the online channel and conda-forge, in that order (--channel, repeated),
with conda's flexible channel priority (the --non-pristine-build pins of (e) and (i) reach the
online channel's build past a rehearsal's local isabelle only under it).  <package> names are conda
package names; the feature that the CONFLICT scenarios (f1), (f2) and the removal (b) work on is
--component when given (light locale today), else --top.  The files those scenarios touch are read
from that feature's patch files in the package cache: (f1) replaces a file that only it changes,
(f2) occupies a path it creates.  The chain is the one (a) installs; (b), (f) and (i) take it from
there.  --strict counts a skipped scenario as wrong unless an empty option asks for the skip
(--other-component, --old-build, --non-pristine-build): the toy channels' runs, where every other
scenario can run.

  (a) isabelle and the top: all applied, one applied line, one heap paragraph, no hook failure
      text, the component registered, the tool runs its own environment's git (win-64: with
      core.longpaths in effect), conda hard-linked the tree from the package cache, the environment
      is of --subdir, --session-dir builds (when given), (k) bytes.
  (b) removing the component package (the user directory stays: isabelle was asked for), then the
      three pruning shapes; (k) after each.
  (c) --force-reinstall isabelle with --other-component installed: still all applied, both registered.
  (d) --clone gives the same state.
  (e) from isabelle at --non-pristine-build with --other-component: installing the component moves
      isabelle to <b>, the chain is applied, the registration is rebuilt.
  (f1), (f2) CONFLICT: one complete diagnostic, the features below applied; --force-reinstall isabelle.
  (g) conda remove isabelle leaves no isa/ (win-64: no target of a patch, no isa.staging, only
      contrib/cygwin/ remains) and no user directory (one with heaps/ in it before); with and
      without feature packages.
  (h) with --old-build: from --start-channel (the online channel: decision 11's one exception) at that
      isabelle build, the update that keeps the release line (`conda install --update-all
      "isabelle==<v>"`, the command texts.not_pristine gives users) moves to <b>.
  (i) a tool-only environment at --non-pristine-build: "no feature packages installed", then exit 3
      (the pristine builds of a version share one ISABELLE_ID; only that build has another).
  (j) (a) in an environment under a git work tree (on win-64 also GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES).
  (l) win-64: status and the tool's whole-chain apply after an unapply (what the post-link of
      --force-reinstall isabelle does under the lock) each hold the lock no longer than 30 s.
  (m) the hooks without a tool and with a tool that cannot start (win-64 also: one that exits with
      a negative NTSTATUS code): the install succeeds, history stays, the pre-unlink's and the
      post-link's hook texts are printed once each.
  (n) win-64: the msvcrt lock (a second status is busy while `type etc\\ISABELLE_ID` and `isabelle
      version -i` work), a read-only patch target, and one held open throughout (Windows refuses to
      replace it; unapply fails cleanly, then converges once it is released).
"""
